#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c78b4a2-6387-513f-b43f-e6d54aec13ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c78b4a2-6387-513f-b43f-e6d54aec13ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f805c-e05b-5b52-9538-96672619b94f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aaf1e23c-5c2a-568b-9798-e760d6ad39ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-351545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0348eb59-0243-542c-b9af-f817342c77ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0348eb59-0243-542c-b9af-f817342c77ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8c31ba-b28d-5c04-adfd-7b5f53d9b579" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d0c74e-4503-59dc-b9ab-8cb0d6da3ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-351595" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccd0312a25669fec949475eaadb042e0c660d094c7579f5bc4f98b68fc9be78d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}+\\frac{3\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
